    {
      "id": "story-frame-17",
      "type": "frame",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 4200,
      "width": 1600,
      "height": 900,
      "name": "Slide 17",
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#202020",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "locked": false,
      "seed": 117,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 1047343975,
      "customData": {
        "title": "My conclusion and learning",
        "ao": "AO4",
        "structure": "build-to-proof bridge",
        "claim": "A promising controlled prototype needs stronger evidence before public deployment.",
        "status": "Conditional conclusion; personal learning and future trials.",
        "sources": [
          "https://app.notion.com/p/3d1d326c13fb800d9fc8e21671d9d3ea — section 8",
          "https://app.notion.com/p/34ad326c13fb80df8615eae09d66a7f7 — retained evidence limits",
          "https://filestore.aqa.org.uk/subjects/AQA-W-7993-SP-19.PDF — sections 2.3 and 2.5"
        ],
        "spoken": "Focaccia gives a promising, limited answer. It shows a way to connect event entry with local face checking and less central face storage, within a prepared prototype. It does not establish readiness for public use. I learned how to connect different parts, trace failures and explain why I made decisions. More importantly, I learned that building something and proving its claims are separate jobs. My next step would be stronger trials and user feedback. That is the conclusion I can defend, and I am happy to take questions.",
        "cue": "Keep the conclusion visible during questions. Ask the supervisor to record the real questions and your actual answers in the Presentation Record.",
        "qa": "[Q&A preparation — not part of the spoken script]\nWhy use a face at all? A face check aims to bind entry to the ticket holder, but I have not proved it is necessary or preferred. A named or ordinary ticket may be simpler.\nIs it completely private? No. Phones still handle face information; the server keeps account and ticket data. The design limits central reusable face records, not every privacy risk.\nWould a photo fool it? I have no retained attack study proving resistance. The present camera check is not validated anti-spoofing.\nWhy only one gate? Disconnected gates cannot instantly share use or cancellation information. One prepared gate kept the project manageable.\nWhat exactly worked? I report the complete journey worked under controlled conditions. Retained dated records support narrower software/browser checks; the full final run lacks a detailed receipt.\nWhat would you change? Freeze features earlier, recruit consenting participants, separate genuine and wrong-person attempts, test photos/videos, record whole-entry time and compare ordinary tickets.\nWas the research original? I did not invent face verification or encryption. I investigated an existing set of ideas in a specific event-entry prototype and evaluated the limits.\nWhat help did you use? Explain the tools, libraries, source material and AI assistance you actually used. This presentation is an AI-assisted draft based on your records; review the language and acknowledge assistance under your centre's rules. Do not claim unaided authorship of these diagrams or this script.\n"
      },
      "index": "b2k",
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793165341252,
      "link": null
    },
